+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report: `lru` 0.18.1
+
+## 1. Setup
+
+- Added `hegeltest` 0.28.2 as a dev-dependency (`cargo add --dev hegeltest --ignore-rust-version`;
+  the crate pins `rust-version = 1.85.0` while hegeltest 0.28.2 requires 1.86 — upstreaming these
+  tests would require the maintainer to accept a dev-dependency MSRV bump).
+- The crate is `#![no_std]` and edition 2015, so the tests need `#[cfg(test)] extern crate hegel;`
+  at the crate root and explicit `use std::{format, string::ToString, vec, vec::Vec, ...};`
+  imports in the test module for hegel-generated code.
+- All tests were added to the existing `mod tests` in `src/lib.rs` (no new files).
+- `.hegel/` added to `.gitignore`.
+- Checked for maintainer AI-consent gates before starting: none found (no `CONTRIBUTING.md`,
+  no `HUMAN_VS_MACHINE.md`, plain MIT license, no anti-AI clause in the README).
+
+## 2. Properties Added (7)
+
+1. **`test_lru_agrees_with_vecdeque_model`** (Tier-1 stateful model test, `#[hegel::state_machine]`):
+   drives `LruCache<u16, u32>` and a reference model (`VecDeque<(K, V)>`, front = MRU, plus the
+   documented capacity) through random sequences of `put`, `push`, `get`, `get_mut`, `peek`,
+   `peek_mut`, `contains`, `pop`, `pop_entry`, `pop_lru`, `pop_mru`, `promote`, `demote`,
+   `resize`, and `clear`. Every rule asserts the return values agree, and an invariant checked
+   after every step asserts: same `len` (and `len <= cap`), same `cap`, identical full MRU→LRU
+   order via `iter()` (which implies same membership and same value per key), and agreeing
+   `peek_lru`/`peek_mru` endpoints. Encodes exactly: `put` on an existing key updates the value
+   AND moves it to MRU; `put`/`push` over capacity evict exactly the LRU entry; `get`/`get_mut`
+   move to MRU but `peek`/`peek_mut`/`peek_lru`/`peek_mru`/`contains` do not; `resize` smaller
+   evicts LRU entries down to the new cap. Keys are drawn mostly from a small pool (0..=7) so
+   operations collide, with the full `u16` range still reachable.
+2. **`test_put_over_capacity_evicts_exactly_lru`**: filling a cache with `cap` distinct keys and
+   putting one more evicts exactly the least-recently-used key; all others survive.
+3. **`test_get_moves_to_mru_but_peek_preserves_order`**: `peek` leaves `iter()` order untouched;
+   `get` moves exactly the touched key to MRU while preserving the relative order of the rest.
+4. **`test_absent_key_ops_are_noops`**: `get`/`get_mut`/`peek`/`pop`/`pop_entry`/`contains`/
+   `promote`/`demote` on a key guaranteed absent (present keys ≤ 255, absent key ≥ 256) return
+   `None`/`false` and do not perturb contents or order.
+5. **`test_capacity_one_holds_exactly_last_put`**: a capacity-1 cache always holds exactly the
+   last-put pair (checked via `len`, `peek`, `peek_lru`, `peek_mru`, and `iter`). Capacity 0 is
+   unrepresentable in this crate — `LruCache::new` takes `NonZeroUsize` — so there is no cap-0
+   case to test.
+6. **`test_resize_evicts_lru_down_to_new_cap`**: `resize` to a smaller capacity keeps exactly
+   the MRU prefix of the pre-resize `iter()` order; `resize` larger keeps everything.
+7. **`test_pop_lru_drains_in_reverse_iter_order`** (sibling-API consistency): after gets shuffle
+   the recency order, repeatedly calling `pop_lru` drains the cache (up to 200 entries) in
+   exactly the reverse of `iter()`'s MRU→LRU order.
+
+## 3. Validation (Run and Reflect)
+
+- **Liveness / mutation check**: applied seven temporary mutations, one per property (model's
+  `get` no longer moves to MRU; inverted eviction assertion; expected-no-move after `get`;
+  inverted absent-`contains`; wrong cap-1 length; expected-no-eviction on resize; dropped the
+  `.rev()` in the drain check). All seven tests failed with shrunk counterexamples — notably the
+  state machine shrank the no-move-on-get mutation to the minimal 3-step sequence
+  `put(0,0); put(1,0); get(0)` failing on "iter() MRU->LRU order disagrees with model". All
+  mutations were then reverted and the suite went green again (the `.hegel` failure database was
+  kept across the broken→reverted runs, then deleted).
+- **Exploratory high-count run**: all seven properties were temporarily run at
+  `test_cases = 1000` (10x default) with per-test `AtomicUsize` case counters printed via
+  `--nocapture` to confirm the count actually ran; every test reported 1000 cases and passed
+  (2.0s wall). Committed tests use the default case count.
+- Final committed state: `cargo test` fully green — 62 unit tests (55 pre-existing + 7 new) and
+  44 doctests.
+
+## 4. Bugs Found
+
+None. No property failed against the real implementation at the default or the 10x case count.
+This copy of `lru` 0.18.1 behaved exactly per its documented contract in every generated
+operation sequence, including the classic LRU trouble spots (recency updates on `put` of an
+existing key, eviction choice at capacity, `get`-vs-`peek` distinction, resize-driven eviction).
+There are therefore no pinned failing tests.
+
+## 5. Limitations & Notes
+
+- The model scans a `VecDeque` (O(n) per op), so machine capacities are drawn 1..=8 (plus a
+  1..=300 branch on `resize`); the bound protects the model's runtime, not any library contract.
+- Not covered: the `get_or_insert*`/`try_get_or_insert*` family, `find_and_promote`,
+  `iter_mut`/`into_iter`, `unbounded()` caches, `Clone`, and custom hashers. The eviction and
+  recency core they share is covered by the model; these would be natural follow-up rules.
+- Memory-leak behavior is already covered by the pre-existing `stats_alloc` tests; the hegel
+  tests exercise values only through `Copy` types, so drop-count checking was left to those.
+- `cargo add hegeltest` requires `--ignore-rust-version` (MSRV 1.86 vs the crate's 1.85); this
+  affects dev-dependencies only.
